      "text": "Let's solve the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the semi-major and semi-minor axes:**\n   The lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse are 10 and 8, respectively. Therefore, the semi-major axis \\(a\\) is 5 and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) is 4.\n\n2. **Calculate the distance from the center to each focus:**\n   The distance from the center to each focus is given by \\(c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Plugging in the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   c = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n   \\]\n   So, the foci of the ellipse are located at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\).\n\n3. **Determine the radius of the circle:**\n   The circle is centered at one of the foci of the ellipse, say \\((3, 0)\\), and is tangent to the ellipse. The radius of the circle will be equal to the distance from the center of the circle to the ellipse at the point of tangency, which is the length of the semi-major axis minus the distance from the center of the circle to the closest point on the ellipse along the major axis.\n\n   The closest point on the ellipse to the center of the circle \\((3, 0)\\) along the major axis is the endpoint of the minor axis at \\((0, 4)\\) or \\((0, -4)\\). The distance from the center of the circle \\((3, 0)\\) to the origin \\((0, 0)\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{(3-0)^2 + (0-0)^2} = 3\n   \\]\n   Since the major axis is along the x-axis, the length of the semi-major axis at the point \\((0, 4)\\) is 5. Therefore, the radius of the circle is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Radius} = a - \\text{distance from center to closest point} = 5 - 3 = 2\n   \\]\n\nSo, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
