      "text": "To find the radius of the circle that is tangent to the ellipse and centered at a focus of the ellipse, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the equation of the ellipse:**\n   The standard form of the equation of an ellipse with a major axis of length \\(2a\\) and a minor axis of length \\(2b\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n   \\]\n   Given \\(2a = 10\\) and \\(2b = 8\\), we have \\(a = 5\\) and \\(b = 4\\). Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^2}{25} + \\frac{y^2}{16} = 1\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the coordinates of the foci:**\n   The distance from the center to each focus is given by \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Substituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   c = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the foci are at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\).\n\n3. **Determine the radius of the circle:**\n   The circle is centered at one of the foci and is tangent to the ellipse. The radius of the circle is the distance from the focus to the point of tangency, which is also the distance from the focus to the boundary of the ellipse that is tangent to the circle.\n\n   The point of tangency of the circle with the ellipse can be found by considering the geometric properties of the ellipse and the circle. The circle is tangent to the ellipse at a point where the distance from the center of the circle to the ellipse is equal to the radius of the circle.\n\n   The maximum distance from the center of the ellipse (which is the origin) to the ellipse along the major axis is the semi-major axis \\(a = 5\\). This distance is also the radius of the circle when the circle is centered at one of the foci. Therefore, the radius of the circle is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{3}\n   \\]",
