      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The given ellipse has a major axis of length 10 and a minor axis of length 8. Therefore, the semi-major axis \\( a \\) is \\( \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\) and the semi-minor axis \\( b \\) is \\( \\frac{8}{2} = 4 \\).\n\nThe distance from the center of the ellipse to each focus is given by \\( c \\), where \\( c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} \\). Plugging in the values, we get:\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n\\]\nSo, the center of the ellipse is at the origin \\((0,0)\\), and the foci are at \\((3,0)\\) and \\((-3,0)\\).\n\nNow, consider a circle centered at one of the foci, say \\((3,0)\\), that is tangent to the ellipse. The radius of this circle will be the sum of the distance from the center of the circle to the point of tangency on the ellipse and the distance from the center of the circle to the focus. This is because the circle is tangent to the ellipse, meaning the shortest distance from a point on the ellipse to the focus is the sum of the radius of the circle and the distance from the circle's center to the focus.\n\nThe point of tangency on the ellipse is the point where the tangent line from the center of the circle is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency. The distance from the center of the circle \\((3,0)\\) to the point of tangency on the ellipse is the radius of the circle plus the distance from the focus to the point of tangency, which is 3 (since the foci are 3 units apart and the ellipse is symmetric).\n\nSince the circle is tangent to the ellipse, the radius \\( r \\) of the circle is the distance from the focus to the point of tangency on the ellipse, which is 3. Therefore, the radius of the circle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
